  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1037 PM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nLATE WEEK INTO SATURDAY STORM:\n\nSatellite and radar imagery shows a strong cyclone off the \nPacNW coast forcing a plume of AR moisture southward across the\nCalifornia and the high Sierras this evening. This plume equates\nto PWAT of 1-2 standard deviations on the wet side of normal or\naround 150-200 percent. As the upstream cyclone moves onshore \ntomorrow remnants of the AR plume will be forced into the \nIntermountain West on strong southerly flow aloft. This moisture\ntranslates into our western CWA by late tomorrow afternoon as \nthe nose of the jet aloft rounds the base of the western trough.\nThe main axis of moisture aligns from SE Utah to our northern \nmountains and peaks tomorrow night before being torn apart and \ntransported eastward. This peak of moisture is concurrent with a\nstrong PV lobe tied to the 130+ cyclonically curved jet aloft.\nThis spreads the best large scale ascent across the northern \nand central CWA through sunrise. This PV lobe also adds \nconditional or perhaps symmetrical instability into the the mix \nwhich hi-res models are utilizing to produce streaky...banded \nprecipitation. This instability shifts eastward on Friday as \nsubsidence spreads in aloft behind the PV lobe lifting into the\nNorthern High Plains. A downturn in precipitation coverage is\nnoted in most models by mid-day. However the GFS has a stronger \nmid level frontogenesis signal from of the end of the Unco to \nthe Flat Tops with a main band of precip reforming Friday \nafternoon that is hinted at in the Euro as well. So confidence \nis not very high overall on prolonged impacts with the \nconvective nature of the precipitation. There remain high \nprobability...over 70%...of 4 to 8 inches over the higher \nwilderness areas of the Elks...West Elks...Flat Tops and Park \nranges. Over the open travel corridors this is much lower and\nhazardous impacts less certain. Persistent moist southerly flow\nsouthern mountains will keep light snow going up high but the \nbest moisture is falling apart by the time the instability \nshifts over Friday. My highest confidence for snowfall is over \nthe higher part of the Eastern Uinta range and will go with a \nwinter weather advisory there. \n\nBy early Saturday a trailing lobe of energy rotates through the\nGreat Basin toward the 4 Corners. QG forcing is impressive \nthrough about mid day across the southern CWA but the AR \nmoisture has been all but wiped out. There could be a burst of \nbetter snow rates and lowering snow ratios over the southern \nmountains during this time but by the afternoon strong \nsubsidence kicks in and this storm will be quickly winding down.\nGenerally 3 to 6 inches looks to fall over the southern\nmountains through the event. Temperatures will be milder in the\npre- storm environment then cool down for Friday into the \nweekend with the passage of the storm. \n\nCOOLER REGIME WITH QUICKLY PASSING STORMS NEXT WEEK:\n\nModels agree on this system shifting the storm track/jet stream\nfarther south over eastern Utah and Western Colorado bringing\nmore unsettled weather to the region through the coming\nThanksgiving week. Expect a disturbance passing through the \nregion every few days with minor impacts to travel mostly \nthrough the mountain areas. Stay tuned for updates as the timing\nof these disturbances become more clear. Daily highs and lows \nlook to hover around five degrees below the seasonal normal \nthrough the week.",
